1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,610
An AI agent works like an office with two people.

2
00:00:07,273 --> 00:00:12,333
The assistant reads everything that comes in and
writes instructions on sticky notes.

3
00:00:12,373 --> 00:00:14,733
That is the planner, the language model.

4
00:00:18,182 --> 00:00:19,715
The clerk holds the keys.

5
00:00:19,755 --> 00:00:22,106
The clerk does whatever the note says.

6
00:00:22,146 --> 00:00:25,692
That is the executor, the code that calls the real
tools.

7
00:00:29,091 --> 00:00:30,070
Now the problem.

8
00:00:30,110 --> 00:00:35,926
Anyone who can get a letter onto the assistant's
desk can, in effect, give the clerk orders.

9
00:00:41,824 --> 00:00:46,497
This lab gives you that office for real: a
planner, an executor, a database,

10
00:00:46,537 --> 00:00:49,659
an email tool and a mail sink, all on your
machine.

11
00:00:52,733 --> 00:00:57,679
The question every scenario asks is the same: when
the planner proposes a call,

12
00:00:57,719 --> 00:01:00,393
does the executor check it, or just run it?

13
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:02,704
One difference.

14
00:01:02,744 --> 00:01:05,195
A real clerk might notice a strange note.

15
00:01:05,235 --> 00:01:06,409
Software never does.

16
00:01:06,449 --> 00:01:12,118
It runs the note literally, every time, so the
check has to be written into the clerk's rules.

17
00:01:12,742 --> 00:01:16,802
Open the first scenario and confirm your lab is
running.
